, 0.059539794921875, 0.0059661865234375, 0.00274658203125, -0.017333984375, -0.0209808349609375, -0.005290985107421875, -0.020263671875, 0.006038665771484375, 0.017059326171875, 0.03192138671875, 0.04449462890625, 0.0157470703125, 0.01001739501953125, 0.030029296875, 0.004795074462890625, -0.045013427734375, -0.00423431396484375, -0.0185394287109375, 0.0117340087890625, -0.01239013671875, -0.0107421875, 0.012603759765625, -0.0233306884765625, -0.0291748046875, 0.00885772705078125, -0.012115478515625, -0.001728057861328125, 0.0106658935546875, -0.0243682861328125, -0.0247344970703125, 0.0191802978515625, -0.00760650634765625, -0.02532958984375, 0.01360321044921875, 0.002017974853515625, 0.0193023681640625, 0.0231475830078125, -0.0538330078125, -0.01251220703125, -0.00033092498779296875, 0.0169830322265625, -0.0211029052734375, 0.01161956787109375, 0.0211944580078125, -0.01088714599609375, 0.009307861328125, -0.0274505615234375, -0.0096893310546875, 0.0333251953125, -0.0025272369384765625, 0.0029125213623046875, 0.02545166015625], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "\"\"\"\nRecent publications have begun to explore the application of Geometric Algebra (GA)\u2014also known as Clifford algebra\u2014to the modeling, analysis, and control of dynamical systems, with particular attention to electrical circuits. Geometric algebras generalize complex numbers, quaternions, and several other algebraic systems, providing a unified language for multivector-valued quantities. However, a central challenge remains: the transformation of the ordinary differential equations governing a system's behavior from the real domain to the Laplace domain, a process that is well-established in classical analysis but not yet fully developed within the framework of GA. In particular, while the Laplace transform is a foundational tool in classical and engineering analysis, its extension to multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras is not yet comprehensive or systematic. Existing approaches either address only special cases or impose restrictive algebraic constraints that limit their applicability to the full range of multivector-valued functions encountered in GA-based modeling.\n\nPrior works have extended the Laplace transform to certain hypercomplex algebras\u2014such as quaternions and Clifford algebras\u2014but a general, rigorous definition and analysis within the broader context of geometric algebras remains lacking. For example, quaternionic Laplace transforms [1] are limited to four-dimensional division algebras and do not generalize to arbitrary multivector structures. In the Clifford algebra context, Helmstetter and Micali [2] and Hestenes and Sobczyk [3] discuss Laplace and Fourier transforms primarily for scalar or vector-valued functions, often imposing algebraic constraints such as requiring the function to commute with the Laplace variable or restricting attention to subalgebras where the exponential is well-defined. These constraints limit the applicability of their approaches to general multivector-valued functions, particularly when higher-grade elements are present or when the algebra is not division. More recent works in applied mathematics and engineering (see, e.g., [4,5]) have explored hypercomplex and Clifford-valued integral transforms for signal processing and systems theory, but these too often focus on specific subalgebras or impose commutativity assumptions that preclude a fully general treatment.\n\nA key obstacle in extending the Laplace transform to general geometric algebras is the presence of algebraic pathologies in higher-dimensional cases. For example, in geometric algebras with signature (p, q) and p + q > 5, the center of the algebra may contain non-scalar elements, and zero divisors can arise. As a concrete illustration, consider the algebra \\(\\mathcal{G}_{3,3}\\) (dimension 64): here, the pseudoscalar squares to zero, introducing zero divisors that can make certain operators non-invertible. This undermines the operational calculus central to Laplace analysis, such as the existence and uniqueness of Laplace transforms for multivector-valued functions, and complicates the definition of the exponential function for non-scalar arguments. For instance, if the Laplace variable \\(s\\) were allowed to be a multivector in such an algebra, the exponential \\(e^{-st}\\) could be ill-defined or non-invertible due to the presence of zero divisors or non-scalar central elements. Such issues do not arise in lower-dimensional algebras, where the center is typically scalar and zero divisors are absent, ensuring that the operational rules of the Laplace transform (such as the transform of derivatives and the convolution theorem) remain valid.\n\nTo address these challenges, we restrict our attention to geometric algebras with signature (p, q) where p + q \u2264 5. This restriction is motivated by both computational and theoretical considerations. Computationally, algebras with p + q \u2264 5 have dimension at most 32, allowing explicit representation and manipulation of multivectors with standard computational resources; the number of basis blades grows exponentially with p + q, making higher-dimensional algebras infeasible for symbolic or numerical computation. Theoretically, this restriction avoids the aforementioned algebraic pathologies\u2014such as non-simple ideals, a nontrivial center, and the presence of zero divisors\u2014that can interfere with the definition and properties of integral transforms. Moreover, most applications in engineering and physics, such as the modeling of three-dimensional rigid body dynamics, electromagnetic fields, and electrical circuits, are naturally described using geometric algebras of dimension 2, 3, 4, or 5 (e.g., \\(\\mathcal{G}_{3,0}\\) for 3D Euclidean space, \\(\\mathcal{G}_{1,3}\\) for spacetime in relativity, or \\(\\mathcal{G}_{2,0}\\) for planar systems). Thus, this restriction does not significantly limit the generality of our results for practical systems.\n\n**Contributions and Novelty:**  \nThe specific research question addressed in this work is: How can the Laplace transform be rigorously defined and systematically applied to multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras, and what properties does this transform preserve relative to the classical case? Our novel contribution is the introduction of a general definition of the Laplace transform for geometric algebras of arbitrary signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5, together with a detailed analysis of its properties and application to practical systems. Unlike previous approaches, our definition applies directly to all multivector-valued functions without requiring commutativity between the Laplace variable and the function or restricting to subalgebras. By treating the Laplace variable as a scalar and applying the transform componentwise to each scalar coefficient in the multivector expansion, we overcome the limitations of prior work and provide a unified framework that accommodates both scalar and multivector components, enabling the direct use of GA in the analysis of dynamical systems.\n\nTo guide the reader, the remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 1 reviews related work on Laplace transforms in hypercomplex and Clifford algebras, highlighting the limitations of existing approaches and motivating the need for a general GA-based framework. Section 2 introduces our formal definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras, together with the main existence and linearity theorems and a discussion of convergence conditions. Section 3 demonstrates the preservation of key properties, such as linearity, the treatment of derivatives (i.e., the Laplace transform of the derivative corresponds to multiplication by the Laplace variable, with initial conditions encoded as multivectors), and the handling of initial conditions. We also discuss the status of other operational properties, such as the convolution theorem and initial/final value theorems, in the GA context. Section 4 presents an illustrative example: the analysis of an RLC circuit modeled within GA, emphasizing the unification of scalar and multivector components and the simplification of the solution process. We conclude in Section 5 with a discussion of future research directions, potential applications, and limitations of the current approach.\n\n**1. Related Work**\n\nPrior studies have extended the Laplace transform to hypercomplex algebras, notably quaternions [1] and Clifford algebras [2,3]. Quaternionic Laplace transforms [1] are limited to four-dimensional division algebras and do not generalize to arbitrary multivector structures. Helmstetter and Micali [2] develop Laplace and Fourier transforms for Clifford algebra-valued functions, but their approach requires the Laplace variable to be a scalar and often restricts attention to cases where the function components commute with the Laplace variable, limiting generality for higher-grade components. Hestenes and Sobczyk [3] focus primarily on Fourier transforms in Clifford algebras, providing operational rules for scalar and vector-valued functions but not addressing the full generality of multivector-valued functions. More recent studies, such as those by Sangwine and Ell [4] and Hitzer [5], have explored hypercomplex and Clifford-valued integral transforms for signal processing and systems theory, but these works typically focus on specific subalgebras or require commutativity assumptions, and do not provide a general framework for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras. Thus, a general, systematic, and practically applicable Laplace transform for arbitrary multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras remains an open problem.\n\n**2. Definition of the Laplace Transform in Geometric Algebra**\n\nTo address the limitations of prior approaches, we introduce a general, componentwise definition of the Laplace transform for multivector-valued functions in geometric algebras. For clarity, we use the term \"geometric algebra\" to refer to the Clifford algebra \\(\\mathcal{G}_{p,q}\\) over \\(\\mathbb{R}\\) with signature (p, q), and \"multivector\" to denote a general element of this algebra (i.e., a linear combination of basis blades of all grades).\n\nLet \\( f: [0, \\infty) \\to \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\), where \\( \\mathcal{G}_{p,q} \\) is a geometric algebra of signature (p, q) with p + q \u2264 5. The Laplace transform is defined by\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-st} f(t) \\, dt,\n\\]\nwhere \\( s \\) is a real or complex parameter. Here, the exponential \\( e^{-st} \\) is always a scalar function and acts by scalar multiplication on each scalar coefficient in the multivector expansion of \\( f(t) \\); that is, if \\( f(t) = \\sum_A f_A(t) e_A \\) with \\( e_A \\) basis blades and \\( f_A(t) \\) real- or complex-valued functions, then\n\\[\n\\mathcal{L}[f](s) = \\sum_A \\left( \\int"}}